{
  "id": "80738",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Percent Ionization",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The strength of an acid can be described by its degree of dissociation, also called "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "percent ionization"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The ratio of the concentration of an ionized species to the initial (nominal) concentration of the species, expressed as a percentage."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "545896838",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3975554078",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " because it is usually expressed as a percentage of the initial acid concentration. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "p3o8sophbkdbpxd",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "dqywe8kp3dj91qk",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "gf30ijmj6jmxads",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/A3/A31F9808B584CB71B1FEA56DD0BCDE4E/green_line.png",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "1my134lqg0l0y77",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "m5f9fl5w4o994mz",
            "items": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The percent ionization of a weak acid is the ratio of the concentration of the ionized acid to the initial acid concentration, times 100%: "
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2675788378",
                    "type": "p"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": " ",
                        "type": "text"
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": true,
                    "src": "\\%\\;\\mathrm{Ionization}\\;=\\frac{\\;\\lbrack{\\mathrm H}_3\\mathrm O^+\\rbrack_{\\mathrm{equilibrium}}} {\\lbrack\\mathrm{HA}\\rbrack_{\\mathrm{initial}}}\\;\\times\\;100\\%",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": " "
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2239794323",
                    "type": "p"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "where [H"
                      },
                      {
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "3"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "O"
                      },
                      {
                        "sup": true,
                        "text": "+"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "]"
                      },
                      {
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "equilibrium"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " is the equilibrium concentration of H"
                      },
                      {
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "3"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "O"
                      },
                      {
                        "sup": true,
                        "text": "+"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " and [HA]"
                      },
                      {
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "initial"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " is the concentration of the weak acid before any ionization occurs."
                      }
                    ],
                    "type": "p"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": " "
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1527559862",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "dd"
              }
            ],
            "title": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": " Percent Ionization of a Weak Acid "
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2654284857",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "type": "dl"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "d9imyknbht6kxwj",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "ezo3ug6t5bn3fqm",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/A3/A31F9808B584CB71B1FEA56DD0BCDE4E/green_line.png",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Because the ratio includes the initial concentration, the percent ionization for a solution of a given weak acid varies depending on the original concentration of the acid, and it actually decreases with increasing acid concentration."
              }
            ],
            "id": "e3vvgmj63q5c6su",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "id": "2230536938",
        "selection": {
          "anchor": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              0,
              0
            ]
          },
          "focus": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              1,
              0
            ]
          }
        },
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Calculate the percent ionization of a 0.125-M solution of nitrous acid, HNO"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", which has a pH of 2.09."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "haurjbevnq7rqgn",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The chemical equation for the ionization of nitrous acid is:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "k4thpabu5og18ln",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "f75g9wl01rx22qq",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "hqtk6e987xkwmhs",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "{\\mathrm{HNO}}_2(aq)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;{\\mathrm H}_3\\mathrm O^+(aq)\\; +\\;\\;\\mathrm{NO}_2^-(aq)",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The initial concentration of HNO"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " is 0.125 M, and we can determine the equilibrium concentration of H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "O"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "+"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " from the pH:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "dzfai075gkkzj8w",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "vdxvpipdt0qnlzy",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "afaex2mkl22sixb",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "\\lbrack{\\mathrm H}_3\\mathrm O^+\\rbrack\\;=\\;10^{-\\mathrm{pH}}\\;=\\;10^{-2.09}\\;=0.0081\\:\\mathrm M",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Using these values, we calculate the percent ionization for this solution of HNO"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ":"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "ov61tx19x3xrlfe",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "uax4jjwkmtwrwef",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "kx120fboqzl2ki6",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "\\%\\;\\mathrm{Ionization}\\;=\\;\\frac{\\lbrack{\\mathrm H}_3\\mathrm O^+\\rbrack_{\\mathrm{equilibrium}}}{\\lbrack{\\mathrm{HNO}}_2 \\rbrack_{\\mathrm{initial}}}\\times100\\%\\;=\\;\\frac{0.0081\\;\\mathrm M}{0.125\\;\\mathrm M}\\times100\\%\\;=\\;6.5\\%",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Remember, the pH value of 2.09 indicates a hydronium ion concentration with only two significant figures."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "p7gzsli0zhhgix3",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
